# STEP 4 -- [PYTHON RUNTIME] ML on the overlap genes, n=42 [MULAI DARI SINIIIIIIIIIII]
**4. Machine learning on a small sample.** To test how the approach
behaves when samples are scarce, 42 samples were drawn at random
(seed 42): 6 Basal and 36 non-Basal. The 79 overlap genes were used as
features. Three models were evaluated with **leave-one-out
cross-validation (LOO)**.

```python
RF  = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=100, max_depth=None,
                             class_weight="balanced", random_state=42)
XGB = XGBClassifier(n_estimators=100, max_depth=3, learning_rate=0.1,
                    scale_pos_weight=36 / 6, eval_metric="logloss",
                    tree_method="hist", random_state=42)
SVM = Pipeline([("scale", StandardScaler()),
                ("svc", SVC(kernel="rbf", C=1, class_weight="balanced"))])
```

Hyperparameters were fixed rather than tuned, because tuning inside LOO
multiplies the number of model fits (about 7x) and overfits easily on
42 samples. Balanced accuracy was used because the classes are
imbalanced (accuracy alone would reward predicting "non-Basal" every
time). Sensitivity and specificity were taken from the LOO predictions.

Significance was assessed with a **permutation test**: labels were
shuffled 50 times, and each shuffle went through the same LOO procedure
to build a chance distribution. The p-value is
(1 + number of shuffles scoring at least the observed value) / 51, so
the smallest possible value is 0.0196. Robustness was checked by
redrawing the 6 vs 36 subsample with 10 different seeds.

For interpretation, RF and XGBoost were fitted on all 42 samples and
explained with SHAP (TreeExplainer), using mean |SHAP| for the Basal
class. Genes in the top 15 of both models formed the consensus list. The
SVM was left out because it was unstable across seeds. Since mean |SHAP|
has no sign, direction was checked separately as the mean z-score
difference (Basal minus non-Basal) on the same 42 samples. SHAP explains
the model fitted on all 42 samples, so it describes what the model uses,
not how well it generalises.
